---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

const title = "Methodology | Texas Home Intelligence";
const description =
  "How Texas Home Intelligence sources, updates, labels, and limits the local data it publishes.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Methodology", url: "/methodology/" }]} />

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:760px;">
      <h1>Methodology</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        How we source, update, and label the local data behind Texas Home Intelligence, and what
        it does and doesn't tell you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:760px;">
      <h2>What this page covers</h2>
      <p>
        Texas Home Intelligence publishes two kinds of content: the free QuoteReady Project Brief
        tool, and a continuously updated layer of local homeowner data and interpretation. This
        page explains how the data layer works.
      </p>

      <h2>Source-first, always attributed</h2>
      <p>
        Every changing figure we publish is designed to state what it measures, its geography, its
        reporting period, when it was last successfully updated, and which public source it came
        from &mdash; for example NWS/NOAA weather data, municipal permit feeds, FEMA flood mapping,
        Texas Department of Insurance loss data, U.S. Drought Monitor drought data, USDA soil
        data, AirNow air quality, Census ACS housing data, BLS trade-wage data, EIA electricity
        pricing, ERCOT grid data, and Texas A&amp;M Forest Service wildfire and tree resources.
      </p>

      <h2>Public / Observed Data vs. Historical Context vs. THI Analysis</h2>
      <p>
        We separate what a source actually reported (<strong>Public / Observed Data</strong>) from
        how that compares to prior conditions or seasonal norms (<strong>Historical
        Context</strong>) from our own plain-language read of what it might mean for homeowners
        (<strong>THI Analysis</strong>). Modeled estimates and anything a homeowner tells us
        directly are labeled as <strong>Estimates &amp; Homeowner Information</strong>, never
        presented as an observed fact.
      </p>

      <h2>Stale and failed data</h2>
      <p>
        If a source feed fails or returns nothing useful, we do not silently show a zero or blank
        value as if it were current. We preserve the last known valid figure and mark it as stale,
        with the date it was last confirmed. If no valid value has ever been obtained, we say so
        explicitly rather than substituting a fabricated number.
      </p>

      <h2>Historical observations</h2>
      <p>
        Where practical, we store historical values rather than overwriting the previous number, so
        a data page can show a trend, not just a snapshot.
      </p>

      <h2>Placeholder data during development</h2>
      <p>
        Some figures across this site are currently marked <strong>SAMPLE</strong> &mdash; these
        are illustrative placeholders for layout and product design only, not real facts, and will
        be replaced by connected live sources before they are presented as current data.
      </p>

      <h2>Not a substitute for professional evaluation</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing on Texas Home Intelligence &mdash; including a generated QuoteReady Project Brief
        &mdash; is an inspection, a diagnosis, or a substitute for a licensed professional&rsquo;s
        on-site evaluation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
